---
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumb from '@/components/Breadcrumb.astro';

const revised = '2026-11-26';
---

<BaseLayout title="About" description="About the Agency–Process–Scale (APS) framework, its intellectual origins, and its scholarly publishing platform.">
  <div class="content-wrapper">
    <Breadcrumb items={[{ label: 'Platform and Policies' }, { label: 'About' }]} />

    <header class="page-header">
      <h1>About</h1>
      <div class="page-revised">Revised: {revised}</div>
    </header>

    <div class="article-body">
      <p>
        The Agency–Process–Scale (APS) framework is a comparative explanatory
        methodology for biology. It defines life as viability-oriented,
        constraint-closed organisation and biological agency as
        viability-oriented organisational activity. Agency, Process, and
        Scale provide complementary analytic perspectives on living
        organisation and its continuity through change.
      </p>

      <p>
        APS seeks to clarify biological concepts, make explanatory
        relationships and dependencies explicit, and facilitate comparison
        between different approaches to biological explanation. Its
        scientific value depends on what it helps researchers clarify,
        connect, investigate, or explain.
      </p>

      <p>
        The APS Publishing Platform presents this work through canonical
        definitions, scholarly articles, historical studies, comparative
        investigations, and ongoing research. It distinguishes conceptual
        clarification and methodological usefulness from demonstrated
        biological explanatory gain.
      </p>

      <h2>Intellectual Origins and Development</h2>

      <p>
        APS developed from botanical, philosophical, and theoretical
        investigations undertaken by Roger Spencer and originally published
        on the website <em>Plants, People, Planet</em>. A central motivation
        was the recognition that plants exhibit organised, adaptive,
        viability-oriented activity without requiring the mental capacities
        ordinarily associated with human agency.
      </p>

      <p>
        These investigations extended into broader questions concerning the
        nature of life, the biological foundations of cognition, knowledge,
        value, and reason, and the relationships between scientific
        concepts and biological reality. They progressively brought
        questions of agency, organisation, temporal continuity, and
        explanatory practice into a common inquiry.
      </p>

      <p>
        An early formal presentation of the framework appeared in:
      </p>

      <p>
        Spencer RD (2025). <em>Biological agency as the context for plant cognition</em>.
        Zenodo preprint.
        <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.16938193" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
          https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.16938193
        </a>
      </p>

      <p>
        Subsequent development has refined the framework's definitions,
        distinguished different kinds of explanatory dependency, and
        established more explicit standards for comparative assessment.
        Earlier formulations form part of its intellectual history rather
        than necessarily representing its current theoretical commitments.
      </p>

      <h2>Scholarly Commitments</h2>

      <p>
        APS does not seek to replace established biological explanations
        or assume the superiority of its own explanatory grammar.
        Claims of additional explanatory gain must be assessed against
        the strongest relevant existing accounts of the same phenomenon
        and supported by appropriate evidence.
      </p>

      <p>
        Conceptual clarity, parsimony, transparent explanatory relationships,
        empirical accountability, and openness to revision are central
        commitments of the framework. Negative and inconclusive comparative
        results are legitimate research outcomes.
      </p>

      <p>
        The platform documents an evolving scholarly inquiry whose
        definitions, arguments, and explanatory claims remain open to
        critical assessment and revision.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>